

-- thick guide: ukca-marking-great-britain (1530 words body, 50 words short answer)

UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyD$# UKCA marking: selling goods in Great Britain

Great Britain (England, Scotland, Wales) has its own product safety regime, and goods placed on the GB market must meet UK statutory instruments, with the UKCA mark where required. Most product areas that required CE marking in the EU have a UK equivalent; the GOV.UK guidance "Placing manufactured goods on the market in Great Britain" is the starting point, and sector-specific regulations define the exact scope. This guide covers when UKCA applies, the marking rules, documentation, and the UK-based parties you need.

## Key facts

- UKCA means UK Conformity Assessed; it is the manufacturer's declaration that the product meets UK product regulations for the Great Britain market.
- Great Britain covers England, Scotland and Wales; Northern Ireland follows different arrangements.
- The UKCA marking must follow the official graphic rules: as a rule it must be at least 5mm in height, visible, legible and indelible, unless the applicable legislation specifies otherwise.
- A UK Declaration of Conformity must be drawn up where the legislation requires it, supported by technical documentation.
- UK "designated standards" give presumption of conformity, mirroring the EU's harmonised standards.
- Under the Product Safety and Metrology (Amendment) Regulations 2024, CE marking continues to be recognised for many product areas in Great Britain; the position is sector-specific.
- Non-UK manufacturers need a UK-established economic operator, such as an importer or authorised representative, in the chain.

## 1. Understand the split

Great Britain (England, Scotland, Wales) has its own product safety regime; Northern Ireland follows different arrangements. Goods placed on the GB market must meet UK statutory instruments, with the UKCA mark where required. The split is regulatory, not just geographic: the same product shipped to Belfast and to Birmingham can face different conformity requirements. Map your destinations explicitly before choosing marking and documentation routes.

## 2. Check whether your product needs UKCA marking

Most product areas that required CE marking in the EU have a UK equivalent. The GOV.UK guidance "Placing manufactured goods on the market in Great Britain" is the starting point; sector-specific regulations define the exact scope. The regimes span product types including electrical equipment, radio equipment, machinery, toys, personal protective equipment, pressure equipment, measuring instruments and others. For each product, identify the applicable UK statutory instrument and confirm whether UKCA marking is required, whether CE recognition applies under the 2024 regulations, or whether Fast-Track UKCA is available. Do not assume: recognition conditions are set per regulation.

## 3. Appoint a UK-based responsible party where needed

Certain regimes require an importer or authorised representative established in the UK who can be contacted by the Office for Product Safety and Standards and market surveillance authorities. If you manufacture outside the UK and place goods on the GB market, your chain needs a UK-established economic operator: typically an importer bringing the goods into the UK, or an authorised representative holding a written mandate. The mandate defines the representative's tasks, such as keeping the declaration and technical documentation available and cooperating with authorities; it cannot transfer your design and production duties. Authorities must always be able to reach a responsible party, so plan for continuity when switching providers.

## 4. Conformity assessment and documentation

UK designated standards play the role harmonised EN standards play in the EU. Keep a UK Declaration of Conformity and technical documentation available to authorities. Conformity assessment routes range from self-declaration (internal production control) to third-party involvement depending on the regulation and product risk. The UK Declaration of Conformity must identify the product, the manufacturer, the UK legislation applied, the designated standards or technical specifications used, and the assessment procedure followed. Technical documentation must demonstrate how the essential requirements are met: design descriptions, risk assessments, test reports and the standards applied. Track the designated standards lists, because UK and EU lists can diverge over time.

## 5. Labelling and traceability

Importer identity, product identification and required warnings must appear on the product or packaging per the applicable statutory instrument. As a minimum, products generally need identification allowing traceability (type, batch or serial number), the manufacturer's name and address, and, for imported goods, the importer's name and contact address. Apply the UKCA marking to the product itself where possible; where the nature of the product makes that impossible, the legislation specifies whether packaging or accompanying documents may carry it. Warnings and instructions must be in English and follow any prescribed formats in the sector legislation.

## 6. Plan for divergence

UK and EU rules can diverge over time. If you sell in both markets, track both regimes: dual marking strategies need ongoing maintenance. Divergence can appear in designated versus harmonised standards, in the scope of recognition, and in sector-specific amendments. Maintain parallel declarations (UK and EU), parallel standards tracking, and a change log that records which regime each product change was assessed against. A product compliant in both markets today needs a process, not just a file, to stay compliant in both.

## UKCA route comparison

| Route | Basis | When to use it |
|---|---|---|
| Full UKCA assessment | UK statutory instruments and designated standards | Where CE recognition does not cover your product, or as a deliberate GB-only strategy |
| CE recognition | Product Safety and Metrology (Amendment) Regulations 2024 | Where your product area is covered and EU requirements are met |
| Fast-Track UKCA | 2024 regulations: UKCA affixed on recognised EU requirements and assessment | Where you want the UKCA mark without duplicating assessment |
| EU-only CE | EU harmonisation legislation | For EU/EEA market placement; does not grant GB access on its own |

## Compliance checklist

- [ ] Confirm the product's applicable UK statutory instruments and marking requirement.
- [ ] Check whether CE recognition or Fast-Track UKCA covers your product area.
- [ ] Map the conformity assessment route: self-declaration or third-party per regulation.
- [ ] Select current UK designated standards for the assessment.
- [ ] Appoint a UK-established importer or authorised representative with a written mandate.
- [ ] Draw up the UK Declaration of Conformity and compile technical documentation.
- [ ] Apply the UKCA marking per the graphic rules (minimum 5mm height as a rule).
- [ ] Add traceability labelling, importer identity and required warnings in English.
- [ ] Set up dual-regime tracking if you also sell in the EU.

## What this means for businesses

For businesses, UKCA is a parallel compliance track, not a copy of CE. The 2024 recognition provisions mean many EU-assessed products can access Great Britain without duplicate assessment, but the paperwork must reflect the chosen route and the position is sector-specific. Budget for a UK-established economic operator, parallel declarations, and ongoing standards tracking. However, note that the EU does not reciprocate: Great Britain-only UKCA marking does not grant access to the EU market, so EU-bound goods always need the full CE process.

## How Great Britain compares with the EU

**Great Britain** uses UKCA marking with UK designated standards and UK Declarations of Conformity, while recognising CE marking for many areas under the 2024 regulations. The **EU** requires CE marking against harmonised standards published in the Official Journal. **Northern Ireland** follows EU rules with the UKNI marking in specific cases. Enforcement in GB is coordinated by OPSS with trading standards; in the EU it runs through the member-state market surveillance framework.

## Does every product need UKCA marking?

No. UKCA applies where the applicable UK statutory instrument requires it. Check the sector-specific regulation; some areas have different or no marking duties.

## What are the UKCA graphic rules?

The marking must follow the official proportions, be visible, legible and indelible, and as a rule at least 5mm in height unless the legislation specifies otherwise. Do not stretch, recolour or rearrange it.

## Can I use my EU technical file for the UK declaration?

Test data can often be reused, but the UK Declaration of Conformity must reference UK legislation and designated standards. Keep a distinct UK file rather than relabelling the EU one.

## Who can be my UK authorised representative?

A party established in the UK holding your written mandate, able to present the declaration and technical documentation to authorities and cooperate with market surveillance. Define the mandate's scope, custody and handover terms in the contract.

## What if my product changes after assessment?

Re-assess: design changes, component substitutions and updates to designated standards can invalidate the earlier conformity work. Keep a change log linking each product revision to its assessment, and repeat the affected tests and documentation updates before placing changed units on the market.

## Assumptions and limitations

- Recognition and fast-track conditions are set per product regulation; verify your sector's position.
- Designated standards lists change; confirm current versions before assessment.
- Northern Ireland, Crown dependencies and British Overseas Territories have separate rules.

## Official sources

- [GOV.UK: using the UKCA marking](https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-ukca-marking)
- [GOV.UK: placing manufactured goods on the market in Great Britain](https://www.gov.uk/guidance/placing-manufactured-goods-on-the-market-in-great-britain)
- [GOV.UK: placing UKCA or CE marked products on the market in Great Britain](https://www.gov.uk/guidance/placing-ukca-or-ce-marked-products-on-the-market-in-great-britain)

Last verified: 2026-09-29.

Plan your next step: [check your product's requirements](/tools/can-i-sell-this) and review your declarations with the [document checker](/tools/document-checker).
