tain';

-- thick guide: ukca-vs-ce-great-britain (1526 words body, 51 words short answer)

UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyD$# UKCA vs CE marking: choosing the right route for Great Britain

After Brexit, Great Britain (England, Scotland and Wales) introduced the UKCA (UK Conformity Assessed) marking alongside the EU's CE marking. The good news for businesses is that the UK continues to recognise the CE marking, alongside or in place of UKCA, for the Great Britain market under the Product Safety and Metrology (Amendment) Regulations 2024. This guide explains how to choose between the routes and what each demands from your supply chain.

## Key facts

- UKCA demonstrates conformity with UK product regulations for the Great Britain market; CE demonstrates conformity with EU harmonisation legislation for the EU and EEA markets.
- Under the Product Safety and Metrology (Amendment) Regulations 2024, the UK recognises CE marking for Great Britain where the aims and requirements of the product regulations meet the UK's needs.
- Fast-Track UKCA allows businesses to affix the UKCA marking where recognised EU requirements and conformity assessment processes have been met.
- The EU does not reciprocate: UKCA marking alone does not grant access to the EU market.
- UK designated standards and EU harmonised standards both give presumption of conformity in their regimes; the presumption is rebuttable in both.
- Northern Ireland follows different rules, including the UKNI marking in some cases; this guide does not cover qualifying Northern Ireland goods.
- Importers or distributors placing products under their own name or modifying them assume the manufacturer's responsibilities.

## What the two markings mean

Both markings are the manufacturer's declaration that the product meets the applicable legal requirements. UKCA demonstrates conformity with UK product regulations for the Great Britain market; CE demonstrates conformity with EU harmonisation legislation for the EU and EEA markets. The regimes span over 20 product types, including goods known in the EU as new approach goods. Northern Ireland follows different rules, and this guide does not cover qualifying Northern Ireland goods.

## Continued CE recognition and Fast-Track UKCA

Where the aims and requirements of product regulations meet the UK's needs, the UK recognises CE marking for Great Britain, which gives businesses flexibility over which conformity process to use. Separately, the same 2024 regulations allow Fast-Track UKCA: businesses can affix the UKCA marking where recognised EU requirements and conformity assessment processes have been met. In practice, many manufacturers serving both markets run one conformity assessment against EU requirements and use the results for both CE marking (for the EU) and UKCA via the fast-track or recognition routes (for Great Britain), subject to sector-specific conditions.

## Designated vs harmonised standards

In Great Britain, designated standards give a statutory presumption of conformity with the relevant essential requirements; they are developed by recognised bodies such as BSI and their use is voluntary in most cases. In the EU, harmonised standards published in the Official Journal play the equivalent role. The presumption of conformity is rebuttable in both systems, and applying a standard never replaces the manufacturer's duty to assess all risks and document how the requirements are addressed. Because the two lists are maintained separately, they can diverge: a standard version giving presumption in one regime may not give it in the other, which affects test plans and declarations for dual-market products.

## Economic operators

Most responsibilities sit with the manufacturer: design and manufacture to the requirements, apply identification details, draw up technical documentation and the declaration of conformity, and monitor compliance. An importer is a business established in the UK bringing products from outside the UK; importers must verify the manufacturer's conformity work, keep technical documentation, and ensure storage and transport do not jeopardise compliance. If an importer or distributor places the product on the market under its own name or modifies it in a way affecting compliance, it assumes the manufacturer's responsibilities. For non-UK manufacturers, the chain needs a UK-established operator, either an importer or an authorised representative holding a written mandate.

## Documentation: UK declaration vs EU declaration

The UK Declaration of Conformity and the EU Declaration of Conformity are parallel documents, not interchangeable ones. Each must reference the legislation of its regime (UK statutory instruments with designated standards, or EU directives and regulations with harmonised standards), identify the product and manufacturer, and describe the conformity assessment procedure used. Test reports can often support both declarations where the underlying requirements align, but each declaration must stand on its own references. Keep both files current: a design change or a standards update may require updating one, the other, or both.

## Labelling and importer identity per route

Whichever route you choose, the product needs traceability identification and the importer's name and contact address where goods are imported. Under the CE recognition route, the labelling follows the EU pattern with the UK importer's details added as the UK legislation requires; under Fast-Track UKCA and full UKCA, the UKCA marking is applied per the graphic rules with the UK declaration behind it. Instructions and safety information must be in English for Great Britain. Keep a labelling specification per route so artwork, declarations and markings stay consistent.

## Route decision table

| Factor | CE recognition route | Fast-Track UKCA | Full UKCA assessment |
|---|---|---|---|
| Marking on the product | CE | UKCA | UKCA |
| Assessment basis | EU requirements and EU conformity assessment | Recognised EU requirements and assessment processes | UK statutory instruments and designated standards |
| Declaration | EU Declaration of Conformity (plus UK route paperwork) | UK Declaration of Conformity | UK Declaration of Conformity |
| Best for | Businesses already CE-marking for the EU | Businesses wanting the UKCA mark without duplicate assessment | GB-focused ranges or sectors outside recognition |
| Watch out for | Sector-specific recognition conditions | Fast-track conditions per regulation | Full UK assessment cost and standards tracking |

## Compliance checklist

- [ ] Confirm the correct market: Great Britain, Northern Ireland, EU, or several.
- [ ] Identify the applicable UK product regulations and whether CE recognition covers them.
- [ ] Choose the route: CE recognition, Fast-Track UKCA, or full UKCA assessment.
- [ ] Map conformity assessment: self-declaration or third-party involvement per regulation.
- [ ] Select designated standards (UK) and/or harmonised standards (EU) as appropriate.
- [ ] Appoint UK-based economic operators: importer, authorised representative, distributor.
- [ ] Draw up the declaration(s) of conformity and compile technical documentation.
- [ ] Apply the correct marking, identification and labelling for each market.
- [ ] Calendar standards-list reviews, because UK and EU lists can diverge.

## What this means for businesses

For businesses, the recognition regime means most EU-compliant products can continue to access Great Britain without a duplicate full assessment, but the paperwork must reflect the chosen route. Do not assume: check each product regulation, because recognition and fast-track conditions are sector-specific. If you place own-brand or modified products on the market, you inherit manufacturer duties, which changes your documentation burden entirely. However, note that the EU does not reciprocate: Great Britain-only UKCA marking does not grant access to the EU market.

## How Great Britain compares with the EU

**Great Britain** accepts UKCA marking and, for many product areas, continues to recognise CE marking under the 2024 regulations. The **EU** requires CE marking and does not recognise UKCA. **Northern Ireland** has its own arrangements, including the UKNI marking in some cases. A product sold in both Great Britain and the EU typically carries CE marking and may also carry UKCA; the declarations and technical files must support each marking used.

## Is CE marking still accepted in Great Britain?

For many product areas, yes, under the 2024 recognition provisions; check your sector.

## What is Fast-Track UKCA?

Affixing UKCA where recognised EU requirements and assessment processes have been met.

## Do I need a UK-based importer or authorised representative?

If you are outside the UK, your products need a UK-established economic operator in the chain: either an importer bringing the goods into the UK, or an authorised representative holding a written mandate. Map the roles so there is no gap in who can present documentation to authorities.

## Can a product carry both UKCA and CE?

Yes, where each marking's requirements are met and each declaration supports its marking. Dual marking is common for products sold in both markets.

## What happens if UK and EU standards diverge?

You may need assessment against different standard versions per regime. Track both designated and harmonised lists and update declarations accordingly.

## Does UKCA work for the EU?

No, the EU requires CE marking.

## Assumptions and limitations

- Recognition and fast-track conditions are set per product regulation; this guide gives the framework, not sector-by-sector answers.
- Northern Ireland, Crown dependencies and British Overseas Territories have separate rules.
- Standards designations change; verify current designated and harmonised standards lists.

## Official sources

- [GOV.UK: placing UKCA or CE marked products on the market in Great Britain](https://www.gov.uk/guidance/placing-ukca-or-ce-marked-products-on-the-market-in-great-britain)
- [GOV.UK: placing manufactured goods on the market in Great Britain](https://www.gov.uk/guidance/placing-manufactured-goods-on-the-market-in-great-britain)
- [GOV.UK: using the UKCA marking](https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-ukca-marking)

Last verified: 2026-09-29.

