-- thick guide: un-38-3-lithium-battery-evidence (1597 words body, 56 words short answer)

UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyD$# UN 38.3 evidence for transporting lithium batteries

Lithium cells and batteries are dangerous goods in transport. Before they may be offered for transport, each cell and battery type must be proved to meet the tests in Part III, subsection 38.3 of the UN Manual of Tests and Criteria, and a test summary documenting the results must be available. Carriers, freight forwarders and authorities will ask for it; without it, shipments are refused. This guide explains the tests, the summary document, and the modal rules that sit on top.

## Key facts

- UN 38.3 is the section of the UN Manual of Tests and Criteria defining design-type tests for lithium cells and batteries.
- Each cell and battery type must pass the full applicable test series before being offered for transport; the summary must cover the specific type, not a similar one.
- The test summary must contain a standardised set of elements, including the UN Manual edition used and a responsible person's attestation.
- Under US rules, each manufacturer and subsequent distributor must make the test summary available.
- UN 38.3 is the common foundation; air (ICAO/IATA), road (ADR), sea (IMDG) and US domestic (49 CFR 173.185) rules add modal requirements on top.
- Lithium ion batteries offered for air transport face state-of-charge limits, and US rules require the watt-hour rating marked on the outside case.
- Damaged, defective or recalled batteries are heavily restricted and generally need special approval.

## What UN 38.3 is

UN 38.3 is the section of the UN Manual of Tests and Criteria that defines the design-type tests for lithium cells and batteries: altitude simulation, thermal cycling, vibration, shock, external short circuit, impact or crush, overcharge and forced discharge. The tests simulate transport stresses to prove the design does not present an unacceptable hazard. A battery pack or module whose primary function is to power equipment is treated as a battery for these purposes. The tests apply whether the cells travel alone, packed with equipment, or contained in equipment; what changes with those configurations is the packing instruction and documentation, not the testing duty.

## The eight tests

| Test | What it simulates | Applies to |
|---|---|---|
| T.1 Altitude simulation | Low pressure in air transport | Cells and batteries |
| T.2 Thermal test | Temperature cycling | Cells and batteries |
| T.3 Vibration | Transport vibration | Cells and batteries |
| T.4 Shock | Mechanical shock | Cells and batteries |
| T.5 External short circuit | Short-circuit fault | Cells and batteries |
| T.6 Impact or crush | Impact (cylindrical cells) or crush (prismatic/pouch cells and batteries) | As specified per cell geometry |
| T.7 Overcharge | Overcharge fault | Rechargeable batteries |
| T.8 Forced discharge | Forced discharge fault | Cells, primary and rechargeable |

A design change can require retesting: if the change could affect the test outcome, the type must be re-evaluated. Keep the test reports linked to the exact design revision they cover.

## The test summary

The test summary is the document that makes the testing visible to the supply chain. Under the US Hazardous Materials Regulations, each manufacturer and subsequent distributor of lithium cells or batteries must make available a test summary, and the requirement applies broadly to cells and batteries in transport. The summary must contain a standardised set of elements: the manufacturer and test laboratory details, a unique test report identification number and date, a description of the cell or battery (type, mass, watt-hour rating or lithium content, physical description, model numbers), the list of tests conducted with pass or fail results, the reference to the UN Manual edition used, and the name and title of a responsible person attesting the information. For example, a power-bank brand must be able to produce the summary for its specific cell model on request from a carrier.

## Modal rules that use UN 38.3

UN 38.3 is the common foundation; each transport mode adds its own rules. For air transport, the ICAO Technical Instructions are the international baseline, implemented operationally through the IATA Dangerous Goods Regulations, with packing instructions such as those for lithium ion batteries packed with or contained in equipment. For US domestic transport, 49 CFR 173.185 sets packaging, marking and documentation rules on top of the UN tests. For road transport in Europe, the ADR applies; for sea, the IMDG Code. Damaged, defective or recalled batteries face severe restrictions and generally need special approval. Check the current edition of the applicable modal code for each shipment, because packing instructions, quantity limits and operator variations change.

| Mode | Baseline regulation | What it adds over UN 38.3 |
|---|---|---|
| Air | ICAO Technical Instructions, IATA DGR | Packing instructions, state-of-charge limits, quantity limits, operator variations |
| Road (Europe) | ADR | Packaging, marking, documentation and vehicle rules |
| Sea | IMDG Code | Stowage, packaging and declaration rules |
| US domestic | 49 CFR 173.185 | Packaging, marking, test-summary availability and documentation |

## State of charge and marking

Lithium ion batteries offered for air transport are subject to state-of-charge limits, and since May 2024 US rules require the watt-hour rating to be marked on the outside case of lithium ion batteries. Packaging must prevent short circuits, damage from shifting, and accidental activation, and packages must carry the applicable hazard communication for their classification. Classify the shipment correctly first: UN 3480 (lithium ion batteries alone), UN 3481 (packed with or contained in equipment), UN 3090 (lithium metal batteries alone) and UN 3091 (packed with or contained in equipment) each lead to different packing instructions.

## Procurement discipline

Never buy lithium cells or batteries without securing the test summary for the exact model: build it into your supplier qualification file alongside the datasheet. When qualifying a supplier, verify the summary's elements are complete, the UN Manual edition is current enough for your carriers, and the model numbers match what you will actually ship. A summary for a similar model does not count, and a missing or incomplete summary will surface at the worst moment, when a forwarder refuses the booking. Include test-summary delivery in the supply contract with an obligation to provide updated summaries after design changes.

## Compliance checklist

- [ ] Confirm every cell and battery type has passed the full UN 38.3 test series for its design.
- [ ] Obtain the test summary from the cell or battery manufacturer for each model.
- [ ] Verify the summary contains all required elements, including the UN Manual edition reference.
- [ ] Classify the shipment: UN 3480, UN 3481, UN 3090 or UN 3091 as applicable.
- [ ] Apply the correct packing instruction, packaging, marks and labels for the mode.
- [ ] Respect state-of-charge limits for air transport.
- [ ] Mark the watt-hour rating on the outside case as US rules require.
- [ ] Train staff performing pre-transport functions in dangerous goods requirements.
- [ ] Segregate damaged, defective or recalled batteries and seek approvals before moving them.
- [ ] Contractually require updated summaries from suppliers after design changes.

## What this means for businesses

For businesses, UN 38.3 evidence is a procurement discipline: never buy lithium cells or batteries without securing the test summary for the exact model, because downstream carriers and distributors will demand it and a summary for a similar model does not count. Build the summary into your supplier qualification file alongside the datasheet. However, note that UN 38.3 covers transport safety only; the EU Batteries Regulation, product safety rules and EPR schemes are separate obligations for placing batteries on the market.

## How the US compares with the EU

The **United States** requires the test summary to be made available by manufacturers and distributors under 49 CFR 173.185, with PHMSA enforcing the Hazardous Materials Regulations. The **EU** applies the same UN 38.3 foundation through ADR for road transport and requires compliance with the EU Batteries Regulation for market placement, including its own documentation and labelling. Air transport follows the ICAO and IATA framework in both markets. Prepare modal documentation per shipment leg.

## Who must hold the test summary?

Each manufacturer and subsequent distributor must make it available.

## Does a similar model's summary cover my battery?

No, the summary must cover the specific cell or battery type.

## Are batteries in equipment exempt from testing?

No, the UN tests still apply; the packing and documentation rules differ.

## What about damaged batteries?

They are heavily restricted and generally need special approval.

## When does a design change require retesting?

When the change could affect the test outcome. Link test reports to exact design revisions and re-evaluate after changes.

## Does UN 38.3 cover batteries I place on the market in the EU?

UN 38.3 covers transport safety. Market placement in the EU additionally requires the EU Batteries Regulation's documentation, labelling and EPR duties.

## Assumptions and limitations

- This guide covers the evidence framework; exact packing instructions, quantity limits and operator variations must be checked in the current IATA DGR, ADR or IMDG Code.
- US rules cited are the federal Hazardous Materials Regulations; carrier policies may be stricter.
- This guide does not cover the EU Batteries Regulation's market-placement duties in detail.

## Official sources

- [PHMSA: transporting lithium batteries](https://www.phmsa.dot.gov/lithiumbatteries)
- [PHMSA interpretation: test summary requirements](https://www.phmsa.dot.gov/sites/phmsa.dot.gov/files/docs/standards-rulemaking/hazmat/interpretations/76876/220044.pdf)
- [EU Batteries Regulation on EUR-Lex (for comparison)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32023R1542)

